#{[17592186286558 :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}\\theta +C"] [17592186286604 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{x\\operatorname{ln}10}-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{log}x"] [17592186286788 :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +C"] [17592186286822 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{x}-\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186286945 :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}x"] [17592186287037 :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}\\theta +C"] [17592186288340 :answer/value "4\\operatorname{csc}x+\\operatorname{tan}x+C"] [17592186288379 :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+C"] [17592186288464 :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{csc}x-\\operatorname{tan}x+C"] [17592186288478 :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x+C"] [17592186288812 :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}x+C"] [17592186288844 :answer/value "4\\operatorname{csc}x+\\operatorname{sec}x+C"] [17592186289171 :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cot}x+\\operatorname{tan}x+C"] [17592186289261 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x+C"] [17592186289264 :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{csc}x+\\operatorname{tan}x+C"] [17592186289350 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+C"] [17592186299291 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186297887] [17592186299462 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186298363] [17592186299861 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4}}{13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x+(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4},\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{4}{y}^{3}-13y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\,(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\,4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\,(x\\,(xy)^{3}-13y) \\\\  & =\\,4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =22x+\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}+f(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{4}{y}^{3}-13y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=22x,\\,f(y)=-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+22x=c.\n$$"] [17592186299862 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3}{\\,2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by$(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+3x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$  when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot 1)-(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =-1-1 \\\\ c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2.\n$$"] [17592186299863 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5}{4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=-2$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot (-2))-2(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =0-8 \\\\ c & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8.\n$$"] [17592186299864 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y{e}^{xy}-5}{4y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-5,\\,Q(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-5) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-4y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=-5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=4$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x & =c \\\\ {e}^{0\\cdot 4}-2(4)^{2}-5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =1-32 \\\\ c & =-31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31.\n$$"] [17592186299930 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}}{3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3+4y\\,{e}^{xy},\\,Q(x,y)=4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =3x+4{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ 4{e}^{0\\cdot 1}-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186299931 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}}{2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y=(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we  rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5},\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+2x=c.\n$$"] [17592186323752 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}=$"]}
